[4] Interpretation
    q=3 gives the known unital pair-frame: 28=C(8,2), block size 4.
    q=6 is only a formal parameter row in finite-geometry terms, but it
    is the clean numerological bridge: h=31, points=7*31, block size 7,
    and cbrt(31) approximates pi because pi^3 is just above 31.
    q=4 gives 65 points; 65+7=72 is a code-length hint, not a construction.
    q=10 gives h=91=C(14,2), echoing the LRC14 pair-slot shell.

